export const HOME_FAQS = [
  { q: "What insurance does a spray foam contractor need?", a: "A spray foam contractor typically needs general liability (with completed-operations coverage), contractor pollution liability for chemical exposures, an off-ratio endorsement or standalone policy, workers' compensation, commercial auto for trucks and spray rigs, tools & equipment coverage, commercial umbrella, and contractor bonds. Most operations carry all eight as one coordinated program." },
  { q: "What is an E&S market and why do spray foam contractors need one?", a: "Excess and surplus lines (E&S) carriers are non-admitted insurers that take risks standard admitted markets won't write. Spray foam contracting is a specialty risk — off-ratio claims, chemical pollution, and completed-operations exposure mean many standard contractors markets exclude or severely limit coverage. E&S markets write the full risk." },
  { q: "What is off-ratio coverage and why does it matter?", a: "Off-ratio refers to spray foam applications where the A and B chemical components are mixed outside the manufacturer's specified ratio. The result can be adhesion failure, odor, off-gassing, or structural damage — and standard GL policies typically exclude the resulting claims. Off-ratio coverage (as an endorsement or standalone policy) is what actually covers these losses." },
  { q: "Does my general liability cover spray foam completed operations?", a: "Only if the policy is properly structured. Standard GL often has low completed-operations sublimits or exclusions for foam-specific perils. We build GL programs with adequate completed-operations limits and make sure the off-ratio and pollution exclusions don't swallow the coverage." },
  { q: "What is contractor pollution liability (CPL) for spray foam?", a: "CPL covers third-party bodily injury, property damage, cleanup costs, and defense arising from the release of pollutants — including isocyanate and polyol compounds, off-gassing during and after spray foam application, and chemical spills. Standard GL excludes pollution. CPL fills that gap." },
  { q: "Does spray foam insurance cover isocyanate exposure claims?", a: "With the right CPL policy, yes. Isocyanate is a known respiratory sensitizer and the primary chemical exposure risk in spray foam work. CPL covers third-party claims for bodily injury from isocyanate off-gassing during or after application. Workers' comp covers crew exposure." },
  { q: "What class codes apply to spray foam workers for workers' comp?", a: "Spray foam applicators typically fall under insulation contractor codes — but the specific code depends on the type of work (residential, commercial, roofing). Correct classification matters: wrong codes mean audit surprises. We assign class codes to your actual operation." },
  { q: "Are spray rigs and proportioners covered under commercial auto?", a: "Vehicles and towed trailers used on public roads need commercial auto coverage. The spray rig, proportioner, and related equipment on the trailer may need an inland marine or tools & equipment floater in addition to auto — we coordinate both so the rig and the equipment are covered." },
  { q: "What does a typical spray foam insurance program cost?", a: "It depends on annual revenue, crew size, job types (residential vs. commercial), loss history, and which lines you carry. Small operations may pay a few thousand per year; larger commercial contractors pay more. We quote your actual operation in about 15 minutes — never a generic estimate." },
  { q: "Can spray foam contractors get admitted market coverage?", a: "Sometimes yes, sometimes no. Admitted markets offer more consumer protections but many will not write spray foam at all or impose broad off-ratio and pollution exclusions. E&S markets typically provide more complete coverage. We shop both and recommend what actually covers your risk." },
  { q: "What is the difference between admitted and surplus lines insurance?", a: "Admitted carriers are licensed and regulated by state insurance departments — claims disputes are backed by state guaranty funds. Surplus lines (E&S) carriers are non-admitted and subject to different regulation; there is no guaranty fund backstop. However, E&S carriers often provide the only complete coverage available for specialty risks like spray foam contracting." },
  { q: "Will a carrier insure a spray foam contractor with prior losses?", a: "Often yes through E&S markets. Declined contractors, those with off-ratio loss history, or those with pollution claims can usually find a market. We have E&S access for spray foam contractors who have been declined by standard carriers or have challenging loss runs." },
  { q: "Do you write spray foam contractor insurance in all 50 states?", a: "Yes. Contractors Choice Agency is licensed in all 50 states and has markets for spray foam operations whether your business is in Texas, Florida, Ohio, California, or anywhere in between." },
  { q: "How fast can we get a spray foam insurance quote?", a: "Typically 15 minutes on a call for standard programs. For larger commercial operations or contractors with complex loss history, we may need a day or two to involve the right E&S markets — but we move fast and tell you the timeline up front." },
  { q: "What subjectivities do spray foam carriers typically require?", a: "Common subjectivities include: manufacturer certification documentation, completed training records for applicators, site safety protocols, proof of PPE compliance, and loss control questionnaires. We help you prepare documentation that supports the best underwriting terms." },
  { q: "Does Lloyd's of London write spray foam contractor coverage?", a: "Yes — Lloyd's syndicates are a significant E&S market for specialty contractor risks including spray foam. We have access to Lloyd's paper through our surplus lines markets when domestic E&S markets aren't the best fit for a specific risk profile." },
  { q: "What triggers coverage under a spray foam GL policy?", a: "Standard GL uses an occurrence trigger for bodily injury and property damage — the coverage in force at the time of the occurrence responds. For completed operations, the key is ensuring adequate limits were in force at the time of the foam application, since claims may come years later." },
  { q: "Should I bundle all my spray foam coverage with one program?", a: "Usually yes. Bundling GL, CPL, off-ratio coverage, workers' comp, and commercial auto into one coordinated program closes gaps between policies and is typically cheaper and cleaner than separate policies from separate carriers — and far easier to manage at claim time." },
  { q: "What GL endorsements do spray foam contractors typically need?", a: "Key endorsements include: off-ratio coverage (or standalone policy), contractor pollution liability, completed-operations extended reporting period, additional insured endorsements for GCs and building owners, and waiver of subrogation. We build the endorsement schedule to match your job contracts." },
  { q: "How do carriers underwrite spray foam risk?", a: "Underwriters look at: annual revenue and job mix (residential vs. commercial), years in business, crew training and certifications, manufacturer authorizations, loss history (especially completed-operations and pollution claims), and the spray systems used. A clean loss run and documented training program are the biggest factors in favorable pricing." },
];

